            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Bengali for to fall out, then the Bengali for a knot of hair."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: অসুখ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "অসুখ — ôsukh — \"an illness\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "অসুখের জন্য অরুণ এক সপ্তাহ স্কুলে গেল না। ôsukher jônyô ôrun ek sôptāhô skule gelô nā. Arun missed a week of school because of illness."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "অসুখ (ôsukh) করা, to fall ill: আমার অসুখ (ôsukh) করল."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "অসুখ (ôsukh) is Sanskrit asukha, \"unhappy, painful\" (Monier-Williams): a-, \"not\", on sukha, the সুখ (ease) of an earlier lesson. Bengali made it the everyday word for an illness, a dis-ease."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing what you lived and felt."
            }
          ]
        },
